## Proof

We seek the smallest positive integer (in decimal) whose digits sum to 2018.

**Step 1: Minimize the number of digits.**

Each digit is at most 9, so an integer with $n$ digits has digit sum at most $9n$. We need $9n \ge 2018$, i.e. $n \ge \lceil 2018/9 \rceil = \lceil 224.22\ldots \rceil = 225$.

So any valid integer has at least 225 digits. Computing $2018 = 9 \cdot 224 + 2$, a 225-digit number can achieve digit sum 2018 by using 224 nines and a single 2.

**Step 2: Among 225-digit numbers, minimize the value.**

For a fixed number of digits, the smallest integer is obtained by making the leading digit as small as possible, then the next digit as small as possible, and so on.

The leading digit must be nonzero. The remaining 224 digits contribute at most $224 \cdot 9 = 2016$ to the digit sum, so the leading digit must be at least $2018 - 2016 = 2$. Thus the leading digit is exactly 2.

With the leading digit fixed at 2, the remaining 224 digits must sum to $2018 - 2 = 2016 = 224 \cdot 9$, forcing every remaining digit to be 9.

**Step 3: Construct and verify.**

The number is $2\underbrace{99\ldots9}_{224}$, i.e. the digit 2 followed by 224 nines.

Digit sum: $2 + 224 \cdot 9 = 2 + 2016 = 2018$. ✓

It has 225 digits (the minimum possible), and among all 225-digit numbers with digit sum 2018 it is the unique smallest (leading digit minimal at 2, all subsequent digits forced to 9). Any number with more digits is larger. Hence it is the smallest positive integer whose digits sum to 2018.

